{
    "unit_title": "Two-Dimensional Motion",
    "chapters": {
        "21": {
            "title": "Projectile motion",
            "summary": {
                "id": 21,
                "key_points": [
                    "A projectile moves under gravity alone (air resistance ignored). Its path is a parabola.",
                    "Treat the motion as two independent parts: horizontal motion at constant velocity $v_x = v_0 \\cos\\theta$, and vertical motion with constant acceleration $g$ downward.",
                    "At the highest point the vertical velocity is zero; the velocity is horizontal and equals $v_0 \\cos\\theta$. The acceleration is still $g$ downward.",
                    "Time of flight depends only on the vertical motion. A ball dropped and a ball thrown horizontally from the same height land at the same time.",
                    "On level ground the range is greatest at $45°$. Angles that add up to $90°$ (such as $30°$ and $60°$) give the same range.",
                    "Doubling the launch speed (same angle) doubles the time of flight and multiplies the range and maximum height by 4.",
                    "In this chapter use $g = 10$ m/s² unless told otherwise."
                ],
                "formulas": [
                    {
                        "label": "Components of the launch velocity",
                        "latex": "v_x = v_0 \\cos\\theta, \\quad v_y = v_0 \\sin\\theta"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Time of flight (level ground)",
                        "latex": "T = \\frac{2 v_0 \\sin\\theta}{g}"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Maximum height",
                        "latex": "H = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin^2\\theta}{2g}"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Range (level ground)",
                        "latex": "R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 2\\theta}{g}"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Horizontal launch from height h",
                        "latex": "t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2h}{g}}, \\quad x = v_0 t"
                    }
                ],
                "body": null
            },
            "solved_exercises": [
                {
                    "id": 210,
                    "problem": "A stone is thrown horizontally at 12 m/s from the top of an 80 m cliff. How long does it take to land, and how far from the cliff does it land? ($g = 10$ m/s²)",
                    "steps": [
                        "Vertical: $80 = \\frac{1}{2}(10)t^2$, so $t^2 = 16$ and $t = 4$ s.",
                        "Horizontal: $x = 12 \\times 4 = 48$ m."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "4 s; 48 m from the cliff.",
                    "sort": 1
                },
                {
                    "id": 211,
                    "problem": "A football is kicked at 25 m/s at $37°$ above the ground. Take $\\sin 37° = 0.6$, $\\cos 37° = 0.8$, $g = 10$ m/s². Find the time of flight, the maximum height and the range.",
                    "steps": [
                        "Components: $v_x = 25 \\times 0.8 = 20$ m/s and $v_y = 25 \\times 0.6 = 15$ m/s.",
                        "Time to the top: $\\frac{15}{10} = 1.5$ s, so the time of flight is 3 s.",
                        "Maximum height: $\\frac{15^2}{2 \\times 10} = 11.25$ m.",
                        "Range: $20 \\times 3 = 60$ m."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "3 s; 11.25 m; 60 m",
                    "sort": 2
                },
                {
                    "id": 212,
                    "problem": "Show that launch angles of $30°$ and $60°$ give the same range for the same speed.",
                    "steps": [
                        "$R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 2\\theta}{g}$.",
                        "For $30°$: $\\sin 60°$. For $60°$: $\\sin 120° = \\sin 60°$.",
                        "The two values are equal, so the ranges are equal."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "Both give $R = \\frac{v_0^2 \\sin 60°}{g}$.",
                    "sort": 3
                }
            ],
