        "22": {
            "title": "Circular and rotational motion",
            "summary": {
                "id": 22,
                "key_points": [
                    "Angles in rotation are measured in radians: one revolution is $2\\pi$ rad. Arc length $s = r\\theta$.",
                    "Angular velocity $\\omega = \\frac{\\Delta\\theta}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = 2\\pi f$. A point at radius $r$ has linear speed $v = \\omega r$.",
                    "In uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the direction changes, so the velocity changes and there is an acceleration towards the centre: $a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r} = \\omega^2 r$.",
                    "A net force towards the centre, $F_c = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$, is needed: friction for a car on a bend, tension for a stone on a string, gravity for a satellite. If it disappears, the object moves off along the tangent.",
                    "Torque is the turning effect of a force: $\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta$. A body is in rotational balance when clockwise and anticlockwise torques are equal.",
                    "Moment of inertia $I$ is the rotational version of mass; it depends on the mass and how far it is from the axis. Rotational kinetic energy is $\\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2$.",
                    "Angular momentum $L = I\\omega$ is conserved when no external torque acts: a skater who pulls her arms in spins faster."
                ],
                "formulas": [
                    {
                        "label": "Angular velocity",
                        "latex": "\\omega = \\frac{2\\pi}{T} = 2\\pi f"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Linear and angular speed",
                        "latex": "v = \\omega r"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Centripetal acceleration",
                        "latex": "a_c = \\frac{v^2}{r} = \\omega^2 r"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Centripetal force",
                        "latex": "F_c = \\frac{mv^2}{r}"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Torque",
                        "latex": "\\tau = rF\\sin\\theta"
                    },
                    {
                        "label": "Rotational kinetic energy and angular momentum",
                        "latex": "K = \\frac{1}{2}I\\omega^2, \\quad L = I\\omega"
                    }
                ],
                "body": null
            },
            "solved_exercises": [
                {
                    "id": 220,
                    "problem": "A 1 000 kg car goes round a flat bend of radius 50 m at 10 m/s. What friction force is needed?",
                    "steps": [
                        "Friction provides the centripetal force: $F = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$.",
                        "$F = \\frac{1000 \\times 10^2}{50} = \\frac{100\\,000}{50}$.",
                        "$F = 2000$ N towards the centre of the bend."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "2 000 N",
                    "sort": 1
                },
                {
                    "id": 221,
                    "problem": "A wheel turns at 120 revolutions per minute. Find its angular velocity and the speed of a point 0.25 m from the axle.",
                    "steps": [
                        "$120$ rev/min $= 2$ rev/s, so $\\omega = 2 \\times 2\\pi = 4\\pi$ rad/s.",
                        "$v = \\omega r = 4\\pi \\times 0.25 = \\pi \\approx 3.14$ m/s."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "$\\omega = 4\\pi$ rad/s; $v = \\pi \\approx 3.14$ m/s",
                    "sort": 2
                },
                {
                    "id": 222,
                    "problem": "A skater spins at 2 rad/s with moment of inertia 6 kg·m². She pulls in her arms, reducing $I$ to 2 kg·m². What is her new angular velocity?",
                    "steps": [
                        "No external torque, so angular momentum is conserved: $I_1\\omega_1 = I_2\\omega_2$.",
                        "$6 \\times 2 = 2 \\times \\omega_2$.",
                        "$\\omega_2 = 6$ rad/s."
                    ],
                    "final_answer": "6 rad/s",
                    "sort": 3
                }
            ],
            "flashcards": [
                {
                    "id": 2200,
                    "term": "Radian",
                    "definition": "The angle for which the arc length equals the radius; $2\\pi$ rad $= 360°$.",
                    "sort": 1
                },
